    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/fort"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The Barrington Atlas Directory notes: Monte Serico. According to Vinson, survey evidence shows that Monte Serico has a long history of human occupation. A Norman castle (tenth century?) now occupies the summit of the hill but archaeological data show that the naturally terraced slopes were inhabited during multiple phases in antiquity. The hill has a commanding view of the Basentello valley and lies near important routes of transhumance. Local pottery and burials of the mid-first millennium BC. A Roman villa was identified on the north slope, but Bronze Age and pre-Roman materials were identified on the same site.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A hill located in the northern part of the modern Basilicata province of Italy. In addition to a prominent Norman castle, archaeological survey indicates human activity stretching back to the Bronze Age. ",
